"Score each option 1-5 on each criterion. There is no NA: a field that says \"none\" or \"none known\" scores 1. Give each score a reason of at most 20 words. Score from the quantities, sources and gaps the option names; ignore verdict words such as COMPUTE-BOUND or NOT COMPUTE-BOUND. Also label each option SAME, ADJACENT or ELSEWHERE against the root question, with a reason: SAME if it asks the root question or a sub-question of it, ADJACENT if it has the root's subject but asks a different question, ELSEWHERE if its subject is different. Decide the subject from the title and summary; count root_link only if the summary itself describes the root question's object of study. Do not add up the scores. Decide only from the text below; do not use tools.
Root question: What limits Bayesian calibration of rainfall-runoff models for river catchments, which needs long MCMC chains? Is compute a major blocker at some level, and could that be fixed with a shit ton of GPU parallelism?
Criteria:
stakes: Suppose the question this option raises were answered, either way. 5 = a choice someone faces (what to buy, build, collect or scale next) turns on the answer, and either answer would move it; 3 = the answer would refine a belief but no choice turns on it, or one answer is already much more likely than the other; 1 = either answer is already expected, or nothing would change. Do not score how famous or important the field is, or how close it is to the root question.
open: Has published work already measured how the answer changes with the quantity compute buys here (samples, ensemble members, resolution, model runs, model size, search width), across the range that $1,000 of Lambda on-demand GPU time (for scale, about 250 H100-hours) can reach? 5 = names the quantity and where published measurements stop (or that none exist), and the unmeasured range starts within that reach; 3 = the unmeasured range is reachable only through a smaller stand-in system, or published work already covers part of the reachable range; 1 = names no compute-bought quantity, or published work already covers everything within that reach, so the open range lies only beyond it.
reshape: Does the option name a part of the work that one answer waits on step after step, and a reason those steps might not have to run in order? 5 = names that chain and roughly how long it is; one answer leaves most of a GPU idle (a small state, or few runs needed, so copies side by side would not fill it); names what would have to be true of the system for the steps to be computed at the same time, in a form a cheap measurement could check; and no cited source already computes this chain in parallel for this system; 3 = names the chain and a way to restructure it, but one of these fails: the answer needs many independent runs, so copies side by side already fill the hardware; one run already fills a GPU; or no checkable condition is named; 1 = sequential says none, or the only parallelism is independent pieces (copies, a sweep, one job per GPU), or a cited source already computes this chain in parallel for this system. A method published for a different system does not lower this score. Do not score how famous the method or field is.
Options, one per line:
{\"id\":\"Single-chain MCMC mixing\",\"title\":\"Single-chain MCMC mixing: the sequential autocorrelation bottleneck\",\"summary\":\"A single Markov chain for a lumped conceptual model must run many serial iterations because posterior samples are autocorrelated; each step conditions on the previous state, and more parallel compute cannot shorten the chain's integrated autocorrelation time, only how fast samples accumulate.\",\"root_link\":\"Bears on the \\"what actually limits Bayesian calibration\\" half of the question — the single chain's autocorrelation is why lumped-model calibration is not compute-bound.\",\"compute_open\":\"none — more parallel compute buys samples-per-second but not a shorter autocorrelation time for a single chain, because τ (equivalently ESS = N/(1+2Σρ_k)) is set by target geometry and sampler, not by hardware (https://mc-stan.org/docs/2_31/reference-manual/effective-sample-size.html).\",\"sequential\":\"One converged posterior waits on one chain whose every step is a proposal from the current state plus a Metropolis accept/reject, and a single GR4J forward run is only a few thousand arithmetic steps per multi-decade daily record (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0022169403002257), yet the chain must run on the order of up to 10^6 generations (DREAM(D)'s default maximum T_max; https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-15-3701-2011) to overcome autocorrelation, and the answer needs only a few independent chains (DREAM(ZS) uses N = 3–5; https://doi.org/10.1029/2011WR010608) for Gelman–Rubin checks, each keeping a tiny fraction of one GPU busy. These steps must run in order because each state conditions on the previous one, so the only way to run them at the same time is to run many independent chains in parallel, which pays off when the target is multimodal and chains are cheap (it buys coverage, not lower τ) — the nearest source that tried it is DREAM (https://research.wur.nl/en/publications/accelerating-markov-chain-monte-carlo-simulation-by-differential-).\"}
{\"id\":\"Ensemble / differential-evolution MCMC\",\"title\":\"Ensemble / differential-evolution MCMC: running many chains in parallel\",\"summary\":\"DREAM-style samplers run N chains simultaneously, generating each chain's proposal from differences of other chains' states; this improves exploration of multimodal/correlated hydrologic posteriors and enables Gelman–Rubin cross-chain diagnostics, and it is the one place where more GPUs change what gets sampled (by raising chain count) without lowering per-chain autocorrelation.\",\"root_link\":\"Bears on the \\"whether a shit ton of GPUs fixes it\\" half — GPUs buy more parallel chains (throughput and multimodal coverage), a real but bounded fix.\",\"compute_open\":\"More parallel compute buys simultaneous chains (ensemble members); standard DREAM runs N = d/2 to d (up to 2d) chains (https://doi.org/10.1029/2011WR010608), DREAM(ZS) needs only N = 3–5, and the largest reported run uses N = 25 chains on a 25-dimensional trimodal target (https://pure.uva.nl/ws/files/1553085/134391_wrcr13130_sup_0002_t02.txt), but it is still unknown whether chains beyond N = 25 further improve posterior coverage or mixing on higher-dimensional, more multimodal hydrology targets.\",\"sequential\":\"none — after burn-in the N chains' joint density factorizes and they are independent, so the ensemble already splits into N chains run simultaneously (https://doi.org/10.1029/2011WR010608).\"}
{\"id\":\"Expensive distributed forward models\",\"title\":\"Expensive distributed / high-dimensional forward models (MT-DREAM(ZS), MGB, SWAT)\",\"summary\":\"For spatially distributed models with hundreds-to-thousands of parameters the forward-model cost dominates the calibration budget, and the workload decomposes into thousands of independent model runs that are embarrassingly parallel across chains and parameter sets; here compute is a genuinely binding constraint that parallelism relieves.\",\"root_link\":\"Bears on the \\"what actually limits Bayesian calibration\\" half — for expensive distributed models the forward-model cost, not mixing, is the binding limit.\",\"compute_open\":\"settled: more parallel compute is already known to bind and pay off here — the forward model dominates the budget (DREAM needed about 50 million surrogate-model evaluations to converge on a 241-parameter groundwater model, https://doi.org/10.1029/2011WR010608), distributed MT-DREAM(ZS) reports \\"spectacular performance improvement\\" on that same 241-parameter problem (https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1029/2011wr010608), and GPU optimization of the MGB model yields up to 20× CPU speedup for calibration and 24× (CPU) / 65× (GPU) for simulations (https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envsoft.2021.105271).\",\"sequential\":\"none — the calibration already decomposes into independent forward-model runs evaluated in parallel across chains and multiple simultaneous proposals, so it is embarrassingly parallel (https://doi.org/10.1029/2011WR010608).\"}
{\"id\":\"Large-sample multi-catchment calibration\",\"title\":\"Large-sample multi-catchment calibration (CAMELS)\",\"summary\":\"Calibrating many catchments (CAMELS provides 671 US catchments) is embarrassingly parallel because each catchment is independently calibratable, so GPU/cloud throughput directly determines how many catchments get a proper Bayesian treatment rather than point optimization.\",\"root_link\":\"Bears on the \\"whether a shit ton of GPUs fixes it\\" half — at CAMELS scale, throughput determines how many catchments can be afforded full Bayesian treatment.\",\"compute_open\":\"More parallel compute buys catchments given full Bayesian treatment instead of point optimization; the largest published set is CAMELS's 671 US catchments (https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017.html), and it is still unknown what extending full Bayesian treatment to all catchments above 671 (or globally) would change about large-sample hydrological conclusions.\",\"sequential\":\"none — the catchments are independently calibratable, so the study already splits into one independent calibration per catchment (https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/21/5293/2017/hess-21-5293-2017.html).\"}

